+/*
+ * The Samsung Exynos990's (Galaxy S20's) heat guard: the CPU temperatures from the APM, a line in the log, and the
+ * phone powered off at a limit. Only with boot-arg exynos_thermal=1.
+ *
+ * Where the temperatures are. On the Exynos990 the temperature sensors (TMU) belong to the APM, a small processor that
+ * runs Samsung's ACPM firmware; Samsung's own kernel does not read the TMU's registers but asks the APM
+ * (drivers/thermal/samsung/exynos_acpm_tmu.c, TMU_IPC_READ_TEMP). So does this driver, through the same message queues
+ * (drivers/soc/samsung/acpm/acpm_ipc.c), polled: the APM's SRAM at 0x02039000 holds the firmware's description of
+ * itself at +0x7000 (ipc_channels at +0x08, ipc_ap_max at +0x18) and, for every channel, its queues; the AP writes a
+ * message of four words into the channel's "rx" queue, rings the mailbox (0x15900000, INTGR0 = bit 16 + channel), and
+ * finds the answer with the same sequence number in the channel's "tx" queue. Channel 9 is the TMU's (the phone's
+ * device tree, acpm_tmu); a request is {0, 0x02 | zone << 16}, the answer's second word has the temperature in whole
+ * degrees in its top byte. Zones: 0 BIG (the Mongoose cores), 1 MID (the A76s), 2 LITTLE (the A55s), 3 G3D (the GPU:
+ * watched before the GPU is ever used, devices/x1s/PLAN.md "GPU").
+ *
+ * What it does with them.
+ *   - every exynos_thermal_poll milliseconds (default 1000) it reads the three CPU zones;
+ *   - sysctl kern.hakios.exynos_thermal_max_c (the hottest), exynos_thermal_zones ("BIG=31 MID=30 LITTLE=30"),
+ *     exynos_thermal_reads;
+ *   - a line in the log at the first reading, every 10 s, and at once when the hottest crosses a multiple of 5 degrees
+ *     upwards from 50;
+ *   - at exynos_thermal_stop degrees (default and most 70: the owner's limit for this phone) on two readings in a
+ *     row it powers the phone off, the way Samsung's kernel does and as HakiOS's U-Boot does on this phone (tested):
+ *     the power chip's restart features (WTSR, SMPL) off through the APM (channel 2, speedy 0, RTC block 0x02,
+ *     registers 0x01 bits 6 and 7, 0x03 bit 3), the APM told to stop (channel 4), PS_HOLD low (PMU 0x1586030C bit 8).
+ *     With a USB cable in, the power chip keeps the phone on: the core then waits with its interrupts off.
+ *   - the GPU has a lower limit of its own: at exynos_thermal_gpu_stop degrees of G3D (default 60) on two readings in
+ *     a row, the GPU's cores are switched off (hakios.mali.c) and the phone powered off the same way;
+ *   - hakios_exynos_temperature(zone) gives other drivers a zone's temperature (the Mali driver reads G3D before
+ *     it powers anything on); the APM's queues are used by one caller at a time (acpm_lock);
+ *   - when the APM gives no temperature for 10 s in a row, the run is handed back to the loader: a guard that cannot
+ *     see is no guard.
+ *
+ * Status: RUN ON THE PHONE (11 Nov 2026): the zones read (BIG=25 ... LITTLE=26 C at rest); with exynos_thermal_stop=20 the
+ * guard decided to power off at 25 C, then panicked on printing with interrupts off; fixed, the next run powered the phone
+ * off (USB cable in; Samsung's charging screen after). Written from Samsung's kernel
+ * source and HakiOS's U-Boot for x1s, where the same queues gave the
+ * temperatures (about 22-26 C at rest) and the same power-off sequence switched the phone off.
+ * Not for any other phone: the addresses are the Exynos990's, and exynos_thermal=1 must not be given elsewhere.
+ *
+ * Boot-args:
+ *   exynos_thermal=1           the guard on
+ *   exynos_thermal_poll=<ms>   how often (200 to 10000; 1000)
+ *   exynos_thermal_stop=<C>    power off at this temperature (20 to 70; 70): lower to test the power-off
+ *   exynos_thermal_gpu_stop=<C> power off when the GPU (G3D) reaches this (20 to 70; 60)
+ */
